Missing: a nested .c-xrefrc below the root is not a project of its own

Locked on the test directory, -getproject for projectB/fileB1.c answers set-info
for the locked project, although projectB/ has a .c-xrefrc of its own and
discovery from that file would find projectB. Expected is project-mismatch, so
the client offers to restart into projectB.

Cause: membership in handleProject() (src/cxref.c) is a prefix test against the
locked root, plus the -I directories. Nothing looks for configs below the root,
and the scan (scanProjectStructure() in src/server.c) does not either, so
projectB's files are also scanned into the enclosing project.

Likely fix: ADR-0031 lets a nested .c-xrefrc below the root prune its subtree.
Put that in the one definition of "own" that scanning and membership share,
rather than in the membership test alone, or the two drift again. A directory
listed explicitly in the config stays own even if it has a config of its own.

Decide first: ADR-0031 is Proposed, and this test pins one of its rules. It is
also the c-xrefactory case itself: locked on the repository, every
tests/test_*/ has its own .c-xrefrc.

The test used to be test_server_project_switch, which switched projects with -p
between two sections of one config. That is the original, pre-ADR-0005 model;
see ADR-0031 for the history.
